92186530348 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b4a256d7-74ef-5f59-aa17-d233ba137369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530454 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt[4]{6}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f72f786-eb31-5b03-9051-ae70858e9f5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530454 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530493 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530493 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95891d65-20e2-5732-9bd4-e52bbf49b5d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530493 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "032c215a-e883-53c2-bdc0-c14d20b9401e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186530457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08362200-3b6f-52bb-9fff-49cbac1aba34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186530454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a2cc474-bbd9-524c-88cd-2cfdbe4a121b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186530493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50c04e39-0863-56d5-a574-6b3bc23fdf01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530563 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186530550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530563 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8d943a17-8038-5310-ba12-619cf9e0f21c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530563 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186530465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186532757 #edb/ref